## Why This Is Asked

This question probes how a candidate designs an actionable, beginner-friendly eval for grounding-heavy QA in a cross-brand setting (Tesla, MongoDB). It tests practicality, grounding fidelity, and the complete flow from data to metrics—not just theoretical understanding.

## Key Concepts

- **Grounding to versioned KB with provenance**: Each claim must reference a specific KB paragraph ID and version, enabling traceability
- **Hallucination taxonomy tailored for product support**: Fabrication (invented facts), misattribution (wrong brand/context), over-generalization (unverified sweeping claims)
- **Relevance to user intent**: Measuring whether responses actually address what users asked
- **Lightweight evaluation scripts**: Minimal, runnable code that beginners can extend

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple grounding verifier sketch
function groundClaim(claim, kb) {
  for (const p of kb.paragraphs) {
    if (claim.includes(p.key)) {
      return { grounded: true, paraId: p.id, version: p.version };
    }
  }
  return { grounded: false };
}

// Hallucination classifier
function classifyHallucination(claim, brand) {
  if (isInvented(claim)) return 'fabrication';
  if (wrongBrand(claim, brand)) return 'misattribution';
  if (isSweeping(claim)) return 'over-generalization';
  return 'valid';
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This task tests grounding and tokenization interplay, a common pitfall when subword vocabularies split referents.

## Key Concepts
- Subword tokenization (BPE)
- Pronoun grounding
- Boundary-aware scoring
- Post-processing to reassemble subwords
- Lightweight evaluation design

## Code Example
```javascript
// Runnable metric: boundary-grounded pronoun accuracy (node.js)
function boundaryGroundingScore(source, summary){
  const stop = new Set(['the','a','an','and','of','in','to','for','with']);
  const isPronoun = t => /^(he|she|they|him|her|his|its|it|this|that|these|those)$/i.test(t);
  const tokenize = s => s.split(/\s+/).filter(w=>w.length);
  const sSent = source.split(/[.!?]+/).filter(x=>x.trim());
  const sumSent = summary.split(/[.!?]+/).filter(x=>x.trim());
  let grounded=0, total=0;
  // naive: consider pronouns in summary and look for uppercase word in source as antecedent
  const srcWords = tokenize(source);
  const ante = srcWords.filter(w=>/^[A-Z][a-z]+$/.test(w));
  sumSent.forEach(sent=>{
    const toks = tokenize(sent);
    toks.forEach((t,i)=>{ if(isPronoun(t)) { total++; const hasAnte = ante.length>0; if(hasAnte) grounded++; } });
  });
  return total? grounded/total: 0;
}

const src = "Alice met Bob yesterday. He explained the plan. The rest followed.";
const sum = "He explained the plan. It was clear.";
console.log(boundaryGroundingScore(src,sum));
```

